Yes. Since this is a Python + FastAPI + scikit-learn project, moving it to another PC is straightforward.

### On the new PC

First install:

* **Git**
* **Python 3.12** (your project was built/tested with Python 3.12)
* Optional: **VS Code / Antigravity / IntelliJ**

Then clone the repository:

```powershell
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/social-engineering-detector.git
cd social-engineering-detector
```

### 1. Create a virtual environment

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
```

Activate it:

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

If PowerShell blocks activation, you can use:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

You should then see something like:

```text
(.venv) PS C:\...\social-engineering-detector>
```

### 2. Install dependencies

Your project already has `requirements.txt`, so:

```powershell
pip install -r backend\requirements.txt
```

If your `requirements.txt` is actually in the project root instead, use:

```powershell
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### 3. Run the application

From the **project root**:

```powershell
uvicorn backend.app.main:app --reload
```

You should get something similar to:

```text
Uvicorn running on http://127.0.0.1:8000
```

Open:

[http://127.0.0.1:8000/](http://127.0.0.1:8000/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

And your application should appear.

### Your complete "new PC" process

```text
Install Git
    ↓
Install Python 3.12
    ↓
git clone <GitHub repo>
    ↓
cd social-engineering-detector
    ↓
python -m venv .venv
    ↓
.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
    ↓
pip install -r backend\requirements.txt
    ↓
uvicorn backend.app.main:app --reload
    ↓
Open localhost:8000
```

### One important thing

Your trained model files (`.pkl`) are important. If they're committed to GitHub, cloning the repository gives the new PC the trained model too, so **you don't need to retrain**.

If you put the `.pkl` files in `.gitignore`, then the new PC won't have the model and we'd need to either retrain or transfer the artifacts separately.

So **before your first push**, I'd specifically check:

```powershell
git status
```

and make sure these are included:

```text
backend/ml/artifacts/
    ├── model.pkl
    └── tfidf_vectorizer.pkl
```

while things like this should generally **not** be committed:

```text
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
```

For your college project, I'd also add a **README.md with these exact setup/run instructions**, so anyone cloning the repo—including your professor or teammate—can understand how to run it.
